  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 127 PM EDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nA weak cold front moved across northern MI this morning. Surface \nobservations show this boundary remaining weak (MSL gradients across \nthe penisula of 1 to 2 mb). Breezy northwest winds will continue \nthis afternoon and evening under strong boundary layer mixing. Above \nour heads, a quasi-PNA pattern exists in the upper levels, placing \nnorthern MI in northwest flow. A jet max will move southward within \nthe upper troughing, placing northern MI in the left exit region \nlate today and tonight. As this embedded wave continues to surge to \nthe south, colder temps aloft reaching northern MI and will result \nin a couple hundred j/kg of MUCAPE starting this evening and lasting \nthrough Thursday. Moisture will originate from the Pacific, and have \ntracked through much of western CAN before reaching MI. Enough will \nexist for light showers (PWATs of 0.6-0.7\"), however accumulations \nfrom any showers this week will remain light (a couple hundredths up \nto a tenth of an inch at a time). \n\nAn intial surge of scattered showers with isolated thunderstorms \nwill be seen early this evening over the eastern U.P. The main \npotential hazard with the strongest storms remains wind gusts up to \n40 to 50 mph at max, since stronger winds exist aloft and DCAPE \nvalues of 500+ j/kg are seen. Small hail will also be possible with \nfreezing levels dropping to 3 to 4 kft tonight (especially over the \neastern U.P). Good to excellent shear will exist during this \ntime (especially 0-6km which could be as high as 50kts at times\nfor certain places). Weak instability and higher shear could \nallow a storm or two to breifly strengthen before it is sheared \noff. A few CAMs do highlight the potential for a few of these \nstorms to strengthen, especially eastern upper and near the tip \nof the mitt (particularly through the straits) this evening.\nThis supports the idea of a storm or two producing stronger\nnear-severe wind gusts. Small hail will be possible, however \nchances remain low for updrafts to be strong enough to sustain \nhail growth. \n\nOff and on showers will continue into tonight for most of northern MI \nwith temperatures cooling into the high 30s and low 40s. Thursday, \nisolated showers with partly cloudy skies will last through the day. \nNorthwest winds will be breezy, with some stronger gusts near NE \nlower coastal locations. Lower chances for hazards with any \nthunderstorms. Friday, surface high pressure and upper level ridging \nwill build in from the west and result in more stable conditions. \nLittle to no precipitation chances, with lighter winds and mild fall \ntemperatures. \n\nStarting Saturday, upper ridging will have shifted over the central-\neastern CONUS. WWA with breezy southwest winds will be seen under \nmostly sunny skies. This warmer airmass will allow temperatures to \nwarm near records for Saturday, reaching into the mid to high 70s \n(possibly low 80s). Warm and dry will likely last into Sunday, \nhowever the plume of of moisture from Isaias could lead to cloud\ncover which would cool temperatures slightly. The moisture \nplume will likely remain to the east of the state, keeping rain \nchances away from northern MI. \n\nRain chances will creep back into the forecast towards early next \nweek, with warmer temperatures lingering through midweek.",
